> Write a Python function that checks whether a number is prime, with a short explanation.

[Start thinking]

The user wants a Python function that checks whether a number is prime, with a short explanation. This is a simple, common programming task. Let me write a clean, correct implementation.

A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.

Basic approach:
1. Handle edge cases: numbers less than 2 are not prime.
2. Check divisibility from 2 up to the square root of n. If any divisor is found, it's not prime.

Why only up to the square root? Because if n = a × b, then at least one of a or b must be ≤ √n. If both were greater than √n, their product would exceed n. So checking up to √n is sufficient.

An optimization: check 2 first, then only check odd numbers (3, 5, 7, ...). Or even better, check divisibility by 2 and 3,

